  "result": "Shell completion now handles `#` correctly. The shell-completion tests pass, including the new ones.\n\n**Cause:** `split_arg_string` in `src/click/shell_completion.py` uses `shlex`, which treats `#` as the start of a comment by default. Everything after a `#` was silently dropped. For example, `cli a#b` was split as `[\"cli\", \"a\"]`.\n\n**Fix:** I set `lex.commenters = \"\"` so `#` is kept as a literal character. The shell has already dealt with comments by the time Click sees the words.\n\n**Tests:** I added `test_split_arg_string_hash` to `tests/test_shell_completion.py`. It covers `a#b`, `#tag` and `a #b`. I did not run the full suite.\n\nI did not add a `CHANGES.md` entry, which CLAUDE.md asks for on user-visible changes. I also did not reproduce the bug against the old code or write the test first, as CLAUDE.md prescribes. I checked `docs/` only by searching the pasted reference pages, which don't mention this behavior.",
